From: Stained Glass OS contributors
Subject: rasapi32, rasdial: VPN connections are sg-netctl's

A Windows VPN client needs a kernel driver (OpenVPN's TAP or ovpn-dco,
WireGuard's wintun) that Wine cannot load, so on Stained Glass OS the tunnel
is the Linux side's: NetworkManager's WireGuard and OpenVPN connections,
which sg-netctl (sg-session) imports from the vendor's own .conf/.ovpn and
lets anyone with a session dial. Here they are what a VPN connection is on
Windows, a RAS phone-book entry:

- RasEnumEntries lists them (all-users entries), RasEnumConnections the
  connected ones (device type "vpn", device WireGuard/OpenVPN), for every
  structure size Windows callers pass; RasGetConnectStatus, RasDial (A/W;
  a notifier hears the outcome), RasHangUp, RasGetErrorString.
- A connection's HRASCONN is derived from its NetworkManager UUID, so
  another process (rasdial /disconnect) names the same connection.
- A password goes to sg-netctl on its standard input: sgnetctl.h's
  sgnet_run_native_input/sgnet_request_input write it to a temporary file
  of the caller's own, handed over as stdin (0078), zeroed and deleted
  after; it is never on a command line.
- programs/rasdial: rasdial [entry [user [password|*]]] [/DISCONNECT], with
  Windows' messages and the RAS error as the exit code.
- Without sg-netctl (a plain Wine) nothing changes: no entries, stubs.

Gate: test/rasdial-gate.sh (a stand-in sg-netctl; 18 checks, 17 fail on
the build without this patch).
